WSNR Experiment No. 9:Ad-Hoc Network Simulation
Aim:
To study and simulate the working of an Ad-Hoc Network by discovering a communication path between nodes and visualizing packet transmission through routing phases (RREQ, RREP, DATA).
Objectives:
• To understand the concept of ad-hoc networking and node communication.
• To implement route discovery between randomly selected source and destination nodes.
• To visualize the route establishment and data transmission in an ad-hoc network.
• To analyze packet forwarding behavior and network connectivity.
Theory:
An Ad-Hoc Network is a decentralized type of wireless network where nodes communicate with each other without the need for any fixed infrastructure or centralized access point. Each node acts as both a host and a router, forwarding packets for other nodes.
Key Features:
- Dynamic Topology: Nodes are mobile and connections change over time.
- Multi-hop Routing: Communication may occur through multiple intermediate nodes.
- Decentralized Management: No central controller is required.

In this experiment, a random graph is created with a fixed communication range. The route is discovered using RREQ flooding (Route Request) and RREP (Route Reply). Once the path is established, data is transmitted along the discovered route.
