Algorithm:
1. Initialize number of nodes and define communication radius.
2. Randomly generate positions of all nodes.
3. Connect nodes that are within the communication range.
4. Select random source and destination nodes.
5. Perform BFS (Breadth First Search) to discover route from source to destination.
6. If a route is found, reconstruct the path.
7. Visualize the network graph.
8. Animate the movement of RREQ, RREP, and DATA packets along the path.
9. Display forwarding nodes for each phase.
Applications:
• Disaster recovery communication systems
• Military tactical networks
• Vehicular ad-hoc networks (VANETs)
• IoT device interconnections without fixed infrastructure
• Smart agriculture and environmental monitoring
Conclusion:
In this experiment, an ad-hoc network was simulated and a route between two nodes was discovered using flooding-based RREQ and RREP packets. The communication path was animated to visualize the packet forwarding behavior. This simulation demonstrates the dynamic and decentralized nature of ad-hoc networks, their multi-hop communication, and importance of routing protocols in such networks.
